  <meta property="og:title" content="What Does PDF Metadata Contain? Every Field Explained (2026)" />
  <meta property="og:description" content="Author, Creator, Producer, XMP stream — every PDF metadata field explained with real examples and risk levels." />
  <meta property="og:url" content="https://fileintel.me/blog/pdf-metadata-fields-explained" />
  <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "What Does PDF Metadata Contain? Every Field Explained (2026)",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-26",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-26",
    "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "FileIntel"},
    "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "FileIntel", "url": "https://fileintel.me"},
    "description": "A complete reference to every PDF metadata field — Info dictionary fields, XMP stream fields, where each comes from, what it reveals, and the privacy risk each carries.",
    "url": "https://fileintel.me/blog/pdf-metadata-fields-explained"
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What fields does PDF metadata contain?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "PDF metadata contains: Author (person who created it), Title, Subject, Keywords, Creator (software used to create the source document), Producer (software that generated the PDF), CreationDate, ModDate, and in some cases Manager and Company from Microsoft Office profiles. Modern PDFs store all of this in two places: the standard Info dictionary and the XMP stream."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the difference between Creator and Producer in PDF metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Creator identifies the application that created the original source document — for example 'Microsoft Word' if you wrote the document in Word. Producer identifies the application that generated the PDF output — for example 'Adobe PDF Library' or 'Microsoft: Print To PDF'. If you wrote in Word and exported directly, Creator and Producer may both say Microsoft. If you later processed the PDF with Acrobat, Producer changes to show Acrobat while Creator stays as Word."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the XMP stream in a PDF?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "XMP (Extensible Metadata Platform) is an XML-based metadata format introduced by Adobe in 2001. Modern PDFs embed an XMP stream alongside the standard Info dictionary — meaning metadata is stored in two separate locations. The XMP stream often contains more fields than the Info dictionary, including revision history and rights management data. Many metadata removal tools only clear the Info dictionary and leave the XMP stream intact."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What does the PDF Author field contain?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The Author field contains the name of the person who created the document, automatically pulled from the operating system or Office account — not typed manually. In Word it comes from File > Options > General > User name. In Google Docs it comes from your Google account display name. In macOS applications it comes from your Apple ID full name. It is personal data under GDPR Article 4(1)."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can you see PDF metadata without special software?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. In Adobe Acrobat Reader (free): File > Properties > Description shows Author, Title, Subject, Keywords, Creator, Producer, and dates. On macOS: right-click > Get Info > More Info. On Windows: right-click > Properties > Details tab. For the XMP stream and all fields, ExifTool (free command line) or FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover show everything in one view."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is PDF metadata personal data under GDPR?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Fields that contain a natural person's name — Author, Manager — are personal data under GDPR Article 4(1). Sharing documents with these fields populated to external parties constitutes a personal data transfer. Under the data minimisation principle (Article 5(1)(c)), this data should be stripped before sharing if it serves no purpose for the recipient."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

